---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
const learning = [
  ['Retrieval', 'Quizzes with varied question types, question banks, randomisation and retake rules, and practice that\'s easy to place throughout a course.', 'Quizzes are absent, or only possible at the end of a course.'],
  ['Assessment', 'Assignments, rubrics, instructor feedback and graded submissions.', 'Multiple choice only, with no feedback.'],
  ['Credentials', 'Configurable certificates and completion rules, with verifiable or shareable credentials such as Open Badges.', 'No certificates, or one fixed template.'],
  ['Learner analytics', 'Per-learner and per-question data, drop-off by lesson, and exports.', 'Enrolment counts only.'],
];
const business = [
  ['Pricing', 'Clear plans, no transaction fees at the tested tier, and fair value for a small creator.', 'Opaque or fee-heavy.'],
  ['Payments', 'Subscriptions, bundles, coupons, payment plans and tax handling.', 'One-off payments only.'],
  ['Marketing', 'Landing pages, email, affiliates and upsells built in.', 'Needs third-party tools for the basics.'],
];
---
<Page
  title="How we review"
  description="The published method behind every Learning Curve platform review: seven criteria, weighted 60% on learning design and 40% on business tools."
  standfirst="Every platform is scored the same way, with learning design weighted above business tools."
>
  <p>
    Most course-platform reviews compare price lists and marketing features. Ours start with a different question:
    how well does this platform help the people taking your course actually learn? Each criterion below maps to
    research in our <a href="/library/">library</a>.
  </p>
  <h2>How we test</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We build a real course on each platform: modules, quizzes, an assignment and a certificate.</li>
    <li>We compare like with like: the cheapest plan that includes certificates.</li>
    <li>Screenshots come from our own test accounts.</li>
    <li>Prices are checked on the date shown in each review.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Learning design: 60% of the score</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">5 means</th><th scope="col">1 means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{learning.map(([c, hi, lo]) => <tr><th scope="row">{c}</th><td>{hi}</td><td>{lo}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h2>Business tools: 40% of the score</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="data">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">5 means</th><th scope="col">1 means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{business.map(([c, hi, lo]) => <tr><th scope="row">{c}</th><td>{hi}</td><td>{lo}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <h2>The overall score</h2>
  <p>
    Each criterion is scored from 1 to 5. The overall score is the average of the learning design scores, weighted
    60%, plus the average of the business scores, weighted 40%, rounded to one decimal place.
  </p>
  <p>
    Scores change only when a platform changes or we test it again; each review shows when it was last updated.
    Affiliate commissions never affect a score. See our <a href="/disclosure/">disclosure</a>.
  </p>
</Page>
